# Rapid Spanning Tree Protocol (RSTP) — Complete CCNA Study Notes

**Based on:** Jeremy's IT Lab — CCNA 200-301 Complete Course, *Day 22: Rapid STP*
**Standard:** IEEE 802.1w (now folded into 802.1D-2004)
**Prerequisite:** Classic STP (802.1D) and PVST+ — Days 20–21 of the course

---

## 1. Why RSTP Exists (The Problem With Classic STP)

Classic STP (802.1D) and Cisco's PVST+ are **reliable but slow**. With default timers, a topology change can take up to **50 seconds** to fully converge:

| Timer | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| Hello Time | 2 sec | How often the root sends BPDUs |
| Forward Delay | 15 sec | Time spent in **Listening** state |
| Forward Delay | 15 sec | Time spent in **Learning** state |
| Max Age | 20 sec | How long a switch keeps BPDU info before considering it stale |

Worst case: **Max Age (20s) + Listening (15s) + Learning (15s) ≈ 50 seconds** of a link being unusable while STP recalculates.

Modern networks (data centers, voice, video) can't tolerate that. RSTP was created to converge in **under a second to a few seconds** in most cases, without abandoning the core STP algorithm (root bridge election, path cost, loop prevention still all work the same way).

---

## 2. The STP Family Tree (Version Comparison)

| Protocol | Standard | Vendor | Instances | Convergence |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **STP** | IEEE 802.1D | Open standard | 1 for entire network (CST) | Slow (~50s) |
| **PVST+** | Cisco proprietary | Cisco | 1 per VLAN | Slow (~50s), but load-balances |
| **RSTP** | IEEE 802.1w | Open standard | 1 for entire network | Fast (<10s, often <1s) |
| **Rapid PVST+** | Cisco proprietary | Cisco | 1 per VLAN | Fast + load-balances (this is Cisco's default today) |
| **MSTP** | IEEE 802.1s | Open standard | 1 per *group* of VLANs (instance) | Fast + scalable |

**Key takeaway:** RSTP is to STP what Rapid PVST+ is to PVST+. Rapid PVST+ is simply RSTP running as a separate instance per VLAN — it's what real Cisco switches run by default (`spanning-tree mode rapid-pvst`).

---

## 3. What Stays Exactly the Same (STP/RSTP Similarities)

RSTP is **not** a totally new algorithm — it reuses almost all of STP's decision-making logic:

- **Bridge ID** (Priority + MAC address) — same format, same tiebreak rules.
- **Root Bridge election** — lowest Bridge ID wins, same process.
- **Root Path Cost** — same concept, cumulative cost to reach the root.
- **Port cost values** — same cost table (see below).
- **Tiebreakers** when costs are equal — lowest sender Bridge ID → lowest sender Port ID → same as STP.
- **One instance per VLAN** when run as Rapid PVST+ — same as PVST+.

What changes is **how fast** ports get from "blocked" to "forwarding," **how many port states/roles exist**, and **how topology changes are communicated**.

---

## 4. Port Costs (Unchanged From STP)

| Link Speed | STP/RSTP Cost (short/default method) |
|---|---|
| 10 Mbps | 100 |
| 100 Mbps | 19 |
| 1 Gbps | 4 |
| 10 Gbps | 2 |
| 100 Gbps | 1 (rounding issue — see "long" method below) |

Because higher-speed links round down to the same low numbers, Cisco also supports the **"long" (32-bit) cost method** for more granularity on very high-speed links, configured with `spanning-tree pathcost method long`. This detail is the same in both classic STP and RSTP — RSTP didn't change port cost math at all.

