## 7. Link Types: Point-to-Point, Shared, and Edge

This is one of RSTP's most important new concepts, because **link type determines whether a port is even eligible for rapid (sub-second) transition.**

| Link Type | Description | Auto-detected from | Eligible for rapid transition? |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Point-to-Point** | Directly connects exactly two switches (or a switch and one other bridge) | Full-duplex mode | ✅ Yes |
| **Shared** | Connects to a shared/multi-access segment (e.g., an old hub) where more than 2 devices could be on the segment | Half-duplex mode | ❌ No — falls back to traditional timer-based STP transition |
| **Edge Port** | Connects to a single end host (PC, printer, server) — never another switch. Conceptually identical to Cisco's legacy **PortFast** feature. | Manually configured (not auto-detected, for safety) | ✅ Yes — transitions to Forwarding **immediately**, no negotiation needed |

**Important nuance:** Link type (point-to-point/shared) is separate from Edge Port status. A port can be point-to-point *and* an edge port (a switch port connected to one PC over a full-duplex link) — in fact this is the most common real-world case.

**Safety mechanism:** If an Edge Port ever receives a BPDU, it immediately loses its edge status and starts participating in normal RSTP calculations (this protects against someone accidentally plugging a switch into what was assumed to be a host port — very similar to how **BPDU Guard**/legacy PortFast behaves, though RSTP's edge-port demotion and BPDU Guard are two separate, complementary safety features).

