## 8. The Proposal/Agreement (Sync) Process — RSTP's Core Innovation

This is the mechanism that actually delivers fast convergence. Instead of a port sitting in Listening/Learning for 30 seconds hoping no loop exists, RSTP **actively negotiates** with its neighbor.

### Step-by-step (new point-to-point link comes up between two switches):

1. **Both ports start as Designated, in the Discarding state.** Neither switch yet knows who's "in charge" of this link.
2. Each switch sends the other a BPDU with the **Proposal bit** set (any Designated port in Discarding or Learning state sets this bit).
3. Each switch compares the received BPDU to its own. The switch with the **worse** (inferior) Bridge ID/path-cost information realizes: *"This port should actually be my Root Port, not a Designated Port."*
4. Before that switch can agree and jump straight to Forwarding, it must guarantee **no loop will form**. It does this via the **sync process**: it puts **all of its other non-edge Designated ports** into the Discarding state temporarily.
   - Ports that are already Discarding, or are Edge Ports, are already "in sync" and don't need to change.
   - If those other ports are themselves point-to-point links to further switches, **they in turn send their own Proposals downstream** — this is why the process is described as "cascading" or "rippling" through the tree.
5. Once the switch has confirmed all of its ports are in-sync, it replies to the original proposal with a copy of the BPDU, but with the **Agreement bit** set instead of the Proposal bit.
6. The switch that sent the original Proposal receives the Agreement and immediately moves that port straight to **Forwarding** — no Listening, no Learning, no waiting on Forward Delay timers at all.
7. This handshake repeats outward, switch by switch, from the root toward the edges of the network — which is why convergence can complete in roughly the time it takes BPDUs to physically propagate through the topology, rather than a fixed 30–50 second timer.

### Why this only works on point-to-point links
The whole handshake depends on there being exactly one other device to negotiate with. On a **shared** segment (hub), a switch can't safely assume "if I get one Agreement, the segment is safe" — there could be other devices still catching up — so RSTP falls back to the old Listening/Learning timer-based approach on shared links.

### If a proposal isn't answered
If a Designated port sends a Proposal and gets no Agreement back (e.g., the neighbor is a legacy 802.1D switch that doesn't understand Proposal/Agreement), the port doesn't hang forever — it simply **falls back to the traditional Listening → Learning → Forwarding progression** with normal Forward Delay timers, exactly like STP.

