## 9. Fast Failure Detection (No More Waiting on Max Age)

Classic STP relies on the **root bridge** to originate BPDUs, and non-root switches largely just relay/refresh them. If a link fails somewhere upstream, downstream switches may have to wait for the **Max Age timer (20 sec default)** to expire before realizing information is stale.

RSTP changes this in two ways:

1. **Every RSTP switch generates and sends its own BPDUs** out every Hello interval (default 2 seconds) on all Designated and Root ports — it does not just wait to relay something from the root. This means link failures are detected locally and fast, not by waiting for a chain reaction back to the root.
2. **Neighbor failure detection:** if a switch fails to receive a BPDU on a port for **3 consecutive Hello intervals (default 3 × 2 = 6 seconds)**, it assumes that neighbor/BPDU information is stale and immediately ages it out — instead of waiting the full 20-second Max Age used in classic STP.

This is what the course refers to as RSTP's **"aging process"** — much faster detection of a dead neighbor without waiting on the old Max Age timer.

