## 11. RSTP BPDU Format Changes

RSTP BPDUs look almost identical to STP BPDUs on the wire, with two key differences — this is the detail you'd confirm by inspecting BPDUs in Wireshark:

| Field | STP (802.1D) | RSTP (802.1w) |
|---|---|---|
| Protocol Version ID | 0 | **2** |
| BPDU Type | 0x00 (Configuration BPDU) | **0x02** (RST BPDU) |
| Flags byte usage | Only 2 of 8 bits used (TC, TCA) | **All 8 bits used** |

### RSTP Flags byte (bit meanings):
| Bit | Meaning |
|---|---|
| 0 | Topology Change (TC) |
| 1 | Proposal |
| 2–3 | Port Role (00 = Unknown, 01 = Alternate/Backup, 10 = Root, 11 = Designated) |
| 4 | Learning |
| 5 | Forwarding |
| 6 | Agreement |
| 7 | Topology Change Acknowledgment (TCA) — kept only for backward compatibility |

Packing the port's role and current state directly into every BPDU (instead of just topology-change flags) is what allows neighbors to make fast, informed decisions without extra round-trips — it's a big part of why the Proposal/Agreement exchange can complete in so few packets.

