## 12. Backward Compatibility With Classic STP

RSTP was designed to be dropped into existing 802.1D networks without breaking them, using **per-port** protocol detection:

- Each port independently tracks whether its neighbor speaks RSTP or legacy STP.
- If a port receives a **legacy (Version 0) STP BPDU**, that specific port falls back to behaving like a classic 802.1D port — full Listening/Learning states, normal Forward Delay timers — while every other port on the same switch can continue running RSTP normally.
- This is sometimes called **Migration Delay** — the switch periodically re-checks whether it can go back to RSTP behavior on that port if it stops seeing legacy BPDUs.

This means you can mix legacy switches into an RSTP network during a migration, and only the links actually touching a legacy switch lose the speed benefit — everywhere else stays fast.

---

## 13. Rapid PVST+ (Cisco's Real-World Default)

Plain RSTP (802.1w) runs **one instance for the whole network** (like CST). Cisco doesn't ship that by default — instead Cisco switches typically run **Rapid PVST+**, which is:

> RSTP's fast port-state machine and proposal/agreement mechanics **+** PVST+'s one-instance-per-VLAN model.

This gives you both benefits at once: sub-second convergence **and** the ability to load-balance traffic by having different VLANs use different root bridges/blocked ports.

