## 15. Worked Example (Conceptual Walkthrough)

Picture three switches, SW1 (Root), SW2, and SW3, connected in a triangle, all links point-to-point/full-duplex:

1. **Root Bridge election:** SW1 has the lowest Bridge ID → SW1 becomes Root. All of SW1's ports become **Designated**.
2. **Root Port election:** SW2 and SW3 each pick their lowest-cost port toward SW1 as their **Root Port**.
3. **Designated Port election:** On the SW2–SW3 segment, whichever switch has the better root path cost (or lower Bridge ID as tiebreaker) becomes **Designated** on that segment; the other becomes **Alternate** (it hears a superior BPDU from the *other* switch, not from itself) and goes to **Discarding**.
4. **Convergence:** Because every link is point-to-point, all of this happens via Proposal/Agreement handshakes almost instantly — SW2 and SW3's Root Ports go straight to Forwarding without ever sitting in Listening/Learning for 30 seconds.
5. **If the SW2–SW3 link later fails and comes back up,** or if SW1 fails: SW2/SW3 re-run the same fast handshake instead of waiting on STP's timers, and the previously-Alternate port can take over quickly.

