## 16. STP vs. RSTP — Quick Comparison Table

| Feature | STP (802.1D) | RSTP (802.1w) |
|---|---|---|
| Port States | 5 (Disabled, Blocking, Listening, Learning, Forwarding) | 3 (Discarding, Learning, Forwarding) |
| Port Roles | 3 (Root, Designated, Blocking) | 4 (Root, Designated, Alternate, Backup) |
| Convergence time | ~30–50 sec | <1–10 sec typically |
| Transition mechanism | Fixed timers (Forward Delay ×2) | Proposal/Agreement handshake on point-to-point links |
| BPDU origination | Root originates; others relay | Every switch originates its own Hello BPDUs |
| Neighbor-down detection | Max Age (20 sec) | 3 missed Hellos (6 sec default) |
| Topology change propagation | TCN hop-by-hop to root, then flood | TC bit flooded directly and immediately by the detecting switch |
| Link type awareness | None | Point-to-point / Shared / Edge, each treated differently |
| Backward compatible | N/A | Yes, per-port fallback to 802.1D behavior |
| Cisco default today | No (legacy) | Effectively yes, via Rapid PVST+ |

